# Execution

SilentRunner's managed execution lifecycle consists of two phases:

- **Prepare phase** -- Validates and resolves execution configuration and prepares
  the output infrastructure and log files.
  The child process has not started yet.
- **Runtime phase** -- Starts and supervises the child process, captures its output,
  and determines the runtime result, including timeout or buffer-limit failures.

Normal completion requires the main child, managed process tree, and both
readers to finish. The reported child exit code comes from the main child;
descendant exit codes are not combined.
Runtime phase is entered only if Prepare phase succeeds. Once the required
infrastructure is available, the finalization procedure concludes either
a failed Prepare phase or the Runtime phase. It handles delayed parent replay,
log finalization and retention, worker shutdown, and post-execution hook launch.
Finalization continues after individual recoverable failures where possible.
Some failures may change the final exit code.

During the Runtime phase, SilentRunner uses a
[Windows Job Object](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/job-objects)
to manage the child process tree, monitor its completion, and terminate it when
necessary. When SilentRunner itself runs inside an existing Job Object, Windows
may form a nested Job Object hierarchy. Restrictions imposed by the outer job may
affect or prevent assigning the child process to SilentRunner's own Job Object.
See Microsoft documentation on
[Nested Jobs](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/nested-jobs)
and
[Assigning Processes to a Job Object](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/procthread/job-objects#assigning-processes-to-a-job-object).

## Execution Modes

SilentRunner supports two execution modes:

### Script/Executable Mode

```text
SilentRunner.exe [SilentRunner options...] <script-or-exe> [child args...]
```

In Script/Executable mode, SilentRunner parses its own options first.
The first non-option argument marks the beginning of the child command.
That argument is treated as the script or executable path, and all
remaining arguments are treated as child arguments.

SilentRunner expands environment variable references (`%VAR%`) in its own
path options (`--cwd`, run hooks, log directories, and `--probe-dir`) during
the Prepare phase. Expansion is performed only once; references introduced
by expansion are not expanded again. References to undefined variables are
rejected. SilentRunner option values (including paths, execution ID
components, and the command string passed through `-c`) containing spaces
must be enclosed in quotes when invoking SilentRunner.

By contrast, the child script or executable command is passed to `cmd.exe`
for resolution and is not pre-resolved by SilentRunner. Environment variable
references in child paths and arguments are also left for `cmd.exe` to
interpret. Its ordinary `%VAR%` substitution is likewise performed only
once per parsing pass. Relative child commands are resolved in the child
working directory, which can be specified with `--cwd`. As with SilentRunner
options, child paths and arguments containing spaces must be enclosed
in quotes.

### Command-string Mode

```text
SilentRunner.exe [SilentRunner options...] -c "<command>"
```

In Command-string mode, `-c` must be immediately followed by the complete command
string as a single argument. Enclose the command in quotes when necessary to
preserve it as one argument.

In both modes, SilentRunner locates the command interpreter using the inherited
`ComSpec` environment variable. During the Prepare phase, its value is expanded
once, resolved to an absolute path, and checked for an existing file. Missing or
empty `ComSpec` uses `C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe` as a fallback, which is also
validated. An explicitly set invalid or undefined `ComSpec` causes a fail-fast
exit code of `255` before child or hook execution; it never triggers the
fallback. The resolved interpreter is reused for child and hook launches.

## Execution ID

Each managed execution has an identifier used to track the run and as the base
name for its log files. By default, it combines a UTC timestamp with the
SilentRunner process ID (`timestamp+pid`). Optional prefix, base, and suffix
components can be configured independently; see
[Execution ID Configuration](parameters.md#execution-id-configuration).

## Exit Codes

On normal completion of the child command, SilentRunner adopts its exit code.
A final exit code of `0` means success; any nonzero value means failure. The
child command runs through `cmd.exe /d /s /c`, so its reported exit code can
also reflect the command interpreter's result.

SilentRunner can produce its own error codes, described below. These are
**SilentRunner-defined codes**: `2`, `124`, `125`, `253`, or `255`.

Code `254` is reserved for a potential no-diagnostics-output failure mode.

Since the child command can also return these codes, the exit code alone
does not always identify whether a failure originated in the child command
or in SilentRunner.

**Phase results and final result:** The Prepare and Runtime phases record
separate exit codes before finalization. The finalization procedure
receives the result of the phase being finalized and can change the overall
exit code if further failures occur. For example, Prepare and Runtime may
both report `0` while a subsequent log finalization failure changes the
final result to `255`.

These results are available in SilentRunner diagnostics under keys:
- `SILENTRUNNER_PREPARE_EXIT_CODE` -- Prepare phase result, when available.
- `SILENTRUNNER_RUNTIME_EXIT_CODE` -- Runtime phase result, when available.
- `SILENTRUNNER_EXIT_CODE` -- Overall exit code, as known when reported in diagnostics or passed to a hook.

The phase-result keys are emitted at DEBUG level, while the overall exit-code key
is emitted at INFO level. The same keys are used as environment variable names
for post-execution hooks.

If the Prepare phase fails, the Runtime phase is skipped and has no phase
exit code. Post-execution hooks may also be skipped if the required
infrastructure has not been prepared.

The `--help` option returns `0` without starting a child command or
invoking post-execution hooks.

During finalization, SilentRunner determines whether to replay delayed parent
output in `success` or `failure` mode. Later errors do not cause this
decision to be reevaluated.

SilentRunner also decides which log files to retain and whether their filenames
indicate `success` or `failure`. If a subsequent error changes the final exit
code, these earlier decisions remain unchanged.

Similarly, diagnostic messages report the exit code known at the time
they are emitted. For example, the `SilentRunner ends` message may report
a different exit code from the one ultimately returned if a later failure
occurs.

### Exit Code 2

**Invalid arguments or configuration.** This includes CLI parsing and
validation errors, as well as an invalid working directory or run-hook
path detected during Prepare. The child command is not started.

### Exit Code 124

**Runtime timeout.** `--timeout-ms` limits process-tree execution and
stdout/stderr draining.
If Runtime has not completed when the timeout expires, SilentRunner aborts
the process tree and stops pending readers with exit code `124`.
After successful process-tree termination, both readers receive one shared
grace period of up to two additional seconds to finish naturally. Remaining
reads are then cancelled.
The grace period is not a strict shutdown deadline. Cancellation of pending
reads may require additional time.
The captured output may be incomplete. A subsequent internal failure,
including an unsuccessful forced-stop operation, may change the final result.

### Exit Code 125

**Replay buffer limit exceeded.** A configured stdout, stderr, or combined
in-memory replay-buffer limit has been reached; execution is aborted. These
limits apply to buffered execution timeline replay rather than compatible persistent
file-based replay. See [Buffering](parameters.md#buffering).

### Exit Code 253

**Post-execution hook launch failure.** SilentRunner could not prepare or
create the process for the selected `--run-on-success` or `--run-on-failure`
hook.

Hooks are launched as detached processes. SilentRunner does not wait for
their initialization or completion and does not monitor their exit codes.

A hook launch failure overrides the previous execution result, including
an internal failure reported as `255`. The success/failure hook selection
is not reevaluated.

### Exit Code 255

Internal SilentRunner failure. Examples include failures in runtime initialization,
process management, output workers, replay, or log finalization. Internal failures
can override the child process's exit code.
